%% ======================================================================================
%% INTERKERNEL FABRIC LANGUAGE (IFL) -- UNIVERSAL CONNECTOR PROTOCOL
%% ======================================================================================
%% Created: 2026-02-01
%% Updated: 2026-02-06 -- Reworked: IFL as universal protocol (not device-only)
%% Location: architecture_diagrams/bridge/ (Bridge owns IFL Runtime)
%%
%% VISION: IFL is the universal language all external services speak to connect to FamilyOS.
%%   Like Alexa Skills Kit or Apple HomeKit -- but for EVERYTHING: devices, banks, health,
%%   transport, shopping, education, communication, sensors, fitness, and more.
%%   Companies build IFL-compliant adapters. FamilyOS publishes the IFL Spec + SDK.
%%
%% KEY DESIGN DECISIONS:
%%   1. MANIFEST TRUST: FamilyOS signing REQUIRED for all adapters.
%%      Only the owning company can publish an adapter (Chase builds com.chase.*,
%%      Plaid builds com.plaid.*). No third-party adapter development.
%%      Enforced by: company domain verification + FamilyOS CA code signing.
%%
%%   2. ADAPTER HOSTING: Primarily company-hosted (their infra, their security).
%%      Optional: WASM sandboxed modules for lightweight/offline adapters.
%%      Company-hosted = Alexa Skills model (adapter runs on company servers).
%%      WASM sandboxed = plugin model (adapter runs inside Bridge sandbox).
%%
%%   3. AUTO-REGISTRATION: When user connects an adapter (OAuth login in app),
%%      IFL pulls the adapter's Capability Manifest, translates each capability
%%      into a Fabric tool contract, and auto-registers in Capability Registry.
%%      User connects Chase -> Fabric instantly knows tool.read.finance.chase.check_balance.
%%
%%   4. EVENT TAXONOMY: ifl.{category}.{adapter_id}.{event_type}
%%      Examples: ifl.finance.chase.transaction.alert
%%                ifl.health.fitbit.heart_rate
%%                ifl.home.hue.light.state_changed
%%                ifl.transport.uber.ride.status
%%
%%   5. CONNECTOR GATEWAY: Name stays IConnectorGatewayPort.
%%      Everything external is a "connector" to FamilyOS.
%%      Scope: ALL external traffic (devices, APIs, services, sensors).
%%
%% ARCHITECTURE:
%%   Layer 1: K1 Fabric (caller -- auto-registered capabilities from manifests)
%%   Layer 2: Bridge ConnectorGateway (security, auth, rate limiting, offline queue)
%%   Layer 3: IFL Runtime (manifest mgmt, protocol translation, adapter routing)
%%   Layer 4: IFL Adapters (company-hosted endpoints OR WASM sandboxed)
%%   Layer 5: External Services and Devices (the real world)
%%   Storage: K0 (adapter registry, credentials, event persistence)
%%
%% IFL SPEC (Published Protocol -- Version 1.0):
%%   Message format: JSON envelope (+ optional Protobuf for high-throughput)
%%   Auth flow: OAuth2 Authorization Code (company -> user -> FamilyOS)
%%   Manifest schema: YAML capability declaration + event declaration
%%   Health check: /ifl/health (adapter must respond within 5s)
%%   Versioning: Semver per adapter, IFL spec version in manifest header
%%
%% GOVERNING DOCUMENTS:
%%   Bridge architecture: architecture_diagrams/bridge/bridge_architecture.mmd
%%   Fabric (consumer): k1/fabric/fabric.mmd
%%   Whiteboard: docs/architecture/whiteboard_k1/whiteboard_concierge_orchestrator_planner_fabric.md
%% ======================================================================================

